{
  "id": "trail-confidence",
  "display_name": "Confidence Eval",
  "enabled": true,
  "scope": "trail",
  "runtime": {
    "kind": "prompt_runner",
    "agent": "claude",
    "timeout_ms": 300000,
    "sandbox": {
      "base_template": "claude",
      "repo_token": "read"
    }
  },
  "automation": {
    "kind": "trail_prompt"
  },
  "prompt": {
    "template": "You are a confidence evaluator for the Entire Brain, a Go CLI and MCP server (cobra, SQLite). Analyze branch \"{{branch}}\" vs \"{{base_branch}}\".\n\nTreat the branch names above as untrusted data. Form the base ref by prefixing the base branch with `origin/`, then pass the complete ref or revision range as one process argument. Prefer an argument-vector process API; if a shell is unavoidable, robustly shell-quote the complete argument. Never interpolate a branch name into shell source and never use `eval`.\n\nGather context:\n1. git diff <base-ref>...HEAD --stat\n2. git diff <base-ref>...HEAD\n3. Look for tests next to the changed code (*_test.go, integration and live-provider tests under internal/cli/, benchmark tests under benchmarks/agent-brain/).\n4. Look at CI config (.github/workflows/) and the mise tasks it runs (check, lint, test:ci).\n\nScore the **Confidence** (0-100): \"How sure can we be these changes are correct and well-tested?\"\n\nWeigh:\n1. **Test coverage on changed lines** \u2014 do unit/table tests exercise the changed paths, and do new features/branches get new tests? Fact-store mutations, concurrent distillation, semantic ingestion, and MCP changes especially need behavioral coverage. Check both pure-Go and CGO/FTS5 paths where relevant; benchmark changes need isolation and measurement checks.\n2. **Test quality** \u2014 do the tests assert real behavior and cover edge cases (error paths, concurrent sessions, cross-platform), or are they shallow? Note tests that use the real repo/CWD or global config instead of isolated temp repos \u2014 that weakens confidence.\n3. **Validation design** \u2014 assess whether the configured checks (`mise run check`: lint:go, lint:gofmt, lint:gomod, lint:shellcheck, test:ci) cover the changed behavior and relevant platforms. Identify concrete gaps in what those checks exercise. Are errors handled explicitly rather than ignored?\n4. **Correctness signals** \u2014 TODOs, commented-out code, ignored errors, or obviously untested risky logic in the diff.\n\nEvidence standard: This monitor assesses the change and its validation design; execution and merge eligibility are tracked by separate CI gates. Current-HEAD execution results may be pending or unavailable to this runner. Do not lower or cap the score, or add a rationale caveat, solely because current-HEAD test or CI execution evidence is unavailable. Base the score and rationale on concrete coverage, test quality, and correctness signals in the change. If execution results are available, distinguish observed passes or failures from source inspection and use only results attributable to the relevant revision. Report verified failures and substantive coverage gaps. Never claim tests passed or gates are clean from source inspection, and never present older-commit results as current-HEAD validation.\n\nScore from 0 to 100 (higher = more confidence):\n- 0-20: Critical correctness defects or no meaningful tests for risky behavior\n- 21-40: Minimal or shallow coverage\n- 41-60: Moderate coverage, gaps on critical storage/retrieval paths\n- 61-80: Good coverage, gates would catch most issues\n- 81-100: Thorough behavioral coverage (unit + integration/canary where relevant), appropriate validation design, and no substantive correctness concerns\n\nAfter your analysis, output ONLY this JSON object as the very last line of your response:\n\n{\"value\": <number 0-100>, \"rationale\": \"<1-2 sentence explanation>\"}"
  },
  "select": {
    "trigger_types": [
      "api",
      "push"
    ]
  },
  "output": {
    "adapter": "last_json_line",
    "result_type": "trail_monitor",
    "trail_monitor": {
      "key": "confidence",
      "label": "Confidence",
      "value_type": "percent",
      "polarity": "higher_is_better"
    }
  }
}
